45417] [17592186562377 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186562182 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562377 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562377 #edb/ref 1077 "field-3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562377 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186562182 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562377 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "ef3a4ab3-90c5-58e4-98ed-cbdc12d7b736" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562421 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186562348 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562421 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186562352 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562421 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186562377 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562421 #edb/ref 1069 "q-335594" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562421 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562421 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=2x(1-0.1x)-xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-3y+xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model with carrying capacity above, where $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators. The equilibria of the model are the following:\n\n$(0,0),\\,(${{field-1}}$,0),\\,(${{field-2}}$,${{field-3}}$)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562421 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3721367d-c322-5ae9-9c82-a2cb18aa384c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562421 #edb/ref 1074 "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2x(1-0.1x)-xy=0 \\\\ -3y+xy=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3y+xy & =0 \\\\ y(-3+x) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $y=0$ or $x=3.$ We consider both cases separately.\n\n- If $y=0,$ substituting it into the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x(1-0.1x)-x(0) & =0 \\\\ 2x(0.1x-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the first two equilibria are $(0,0)$ and $(10,0).$\n\n- If $x=3,$ substituting it into the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(3)(1-0.1(3))-(3)y & =0 \\\\ 4.2-3y & =0 \\\\ y & =1.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, another equilibrium is $(3,1.4).$\n\nTherefore, the equilibria of the model are\n\n$$\n(0,0),\\,(10,0),\\,(3,1.4).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562503 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186562228 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562503 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186562256 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562503 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186562262 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562503 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186562264 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562503 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186562276 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562503 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186562299 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562503 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186562323 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562503 #edb/ref 1069 "q-334872" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562503 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562503 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=2x(1-x)-xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-2y+4xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model with carrying capacity above, where $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\n$\\,$ Evaluating the Jacobian of the system at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[0.5,\\,1]^{T},$ we get\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$J({x}^{∗})=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,.$</td>\n</tr><tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\n$\\,$ The eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix are \n{{field-5}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the equilibrium at ${x}^{∗}$ is \n{{field-6}}{{field-7}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562503 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e335f33f-c98e-5cab-bfc9-af13ddc0ea7b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562503 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's denote ${f}_{1}(x,y)=2x(1-x)-xy$ and ${f}_{2}(x,y)=-2y+4xy.$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x(1-x)-xy)=2-4x-y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x(1-x)-xy)=-x \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-2y+4xy)=4y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-2y+4xy)=-2+4x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the Jacobian matrix is\n\n$$\nJ=[\\begin{aligned}2-4x-y & -x \\\\ 4y & -2+4x\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[\\begin{aligned}0.5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ to get\n\n$$\nJ({x}^{∗})=[\\begin{aligned}2-4(0.5)-(1) & -(0.5) \\\\ 4(1) & -2+4(0.5)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -0.5 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix, we write down the characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}-1-\\lambda  & -0.5 \\\\ 4 & -\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )(-\\lambda )-4\\cdot (-0.5) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda +2 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the equation, we get the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1,2}=-0.5\\pm 0.5\\sqrt{7}\\,\\text{i}.$ Since the eigenvalues are complex with negative real parts, our equilibrium is $\\text{an asymptotically stable}$ $\\text{spiral sink}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562554 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186562555 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562554 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7f546ceb-808c-5bc5-becd-cf02fd535255" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562554 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186562503 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562555 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186562564 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562555 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "81c1df34-ad18-5252-bc8d-00804c2ca251" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562555 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186167254 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562564 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ad127185-be66-5e16-bb79-eea3f103e5a6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562564 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186562421 #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
